.TH POTATOMAXX 1 "2026-08-22" "potatomaxx 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
potatomaxx \- make mixture-of-experts models cheaper to read
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B potatomaxx
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A large mixture-of-experts (MoE) checkpoint does not fit in RAM, so it is read
from storage while it runs. That makes decoding a storage problem.
.B potatomaxx
reduces the cost of those reads three ways, and measures each rather than
assuming it: by reordering experts on disk so co-firing experts are adjacent, by
storing each expert at its own bit width, and by predicting which experts a layer
will select before its router has run.
.PP
The layout pass emits a
.B drop-in GGUF
\(em same tensor names, same shapes, byte-identical weights \(em so any engine
reads it unchanged. Permuting the expert axis is a relabelling: permute the
router's rows identically and the computed function is unchanged bit for bit.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B probe
Measure this device's read bandwidth surface (request size against queue depth)
using O_DIRECT, and write it as JSON for the other commands to cost against.
.TP
.B kio
Compare kernel I/O paths on this machine: synchronous
.BR pread (2),
a thread pool, and io_uring in both batch and sliding-window form, with and
without
.BR RWF_DONTCACHE .
Reports page-cache residency alongside bandwidth and recommends an engine from
the measurement.
.TP
.B synth
Write a small synthetic MoE checkpoint and routing trace, so the whole pipeline
can be exercised without downloading a model.
.TP
.B inspect \fIMODEL\fR
Report the MoE structure of a GGUF checkpoint: experts per layer, slice size,
precision, and which tensors carry the expert axis.
.TP
.B analyse
Score the checkpoint's existing expert order against an optimised one, per layer,
costed against a measured bandwidth surface. Says plainly when the gain is not
worth rewriting a file for.
.TP
.B plan
Write a reviewable text plan describing exactly which tensors move and how.
.TP
.B pack
Apply a plan, emitting a new GGUF.
.TP
.B verify
Prove a repacked file holds exactly the original weights, by comparing both files
in full. Strict enough to reject a single flipped byte.
.TP
.B predict
Compare router-lookahead predictors on a trace, fitting on a prefix and scoring on
held-out tokens.
.TP
.B build-store
Requantise a checkpoint per expert into a native
.IR .pmxstore ,
allocating bits from measured per-expert error and access frequency. Routers are
never requantised.
.TP
.B bench
Replay a trace against a store, measuring the expert-fetch path: cache hit rate,
bytes and requests per token, and fetch-limited throughput.
.SH OPTIONS
Options are per-command; run
.B potatomaxx help
for the full list. Commonly useful:
.TP
.BR \-\-probe " \fIFILE\fR"
Cost against a bandwidth surface written by
.BR "potatomaxx probe" .
Without it, only request counts are reported, never times.
.TP
.BR \-\-min\-speedup " \fIF\fR"
Gain a layer must clear to be judged worth repacking. Default 1.05.
.TP
.BR \-\-error\-bits " \fIF\fR"
Quality ceiling for precision allocation, expressed as the bit width whose
uniform error must not be exceeded. Lower is more aggressive.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero on success, non-zero on error. A refusal to write a plan because no layer
clears the threshold is a
.B success:
"no useful gain here" is an answer, not a failure.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I *.pmxtrace
Routing trace. A text form is accepted: one line per token and layer,
.RI \(dq token " " layer " " experts... \(dq.
.TP
.I *.pmxplan
Repack plan. Plain text, so it can be reviewed before a multi-gigabyte file is
rewritten.
.TP
.I *.pmxstore
Native expert store with per-expert precision, which GGUF cannot express because
a tensor carries one type and a layer's experts share a tensor.
.SH NOTES
.B bench
reports a ceiling on decode rate, not a decode rate: it moves expert weights but
does not run attention or sampling. On a memory-bound machine that ceiling is the
binding constraint.
.PP
The kernel I/O paths need Linux. On other targets every syscall wrapper fails
closed with ENOSYS and no syscall is issued.
.SH SECURITY
Model files are untrusted input. Every length and offset read from a file is
validated with checked arithmetic against the real file size. See
.I SECURITY.md
in the distribution.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fio (1),
.BR io_uring (7),
.BR madvise (2),
.BR cachestat (2)
.PP
Full manual:
.B info potatomaxx
